-- title:
--   Branch quotient of the uv=π crossing model
-- statement:
--   Let $W$ be a commutative ring and let $\pi \in W$. Write $\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi$ for the quotient of the two-variable power series ring $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}(\mathrm{Fin}\ 2)\ W$ by the ideal generated by the single element $X_0 X_1 - C(\pi)$, and let `U π` and `V π` denote the two distinguished branch coordinates of this model (elements of it attached to the two variables), and for $w \in W$ let `const π w` be the class of the constant power series $C(w)$. The theorem asserts the existence of a ring isomorphism
--   $$e : \bigl(\mathrm{UVCrossingModel}\ W\ \pi\bigr)\big/ (U_\pi) \;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\; \bigl(W/(\pi)\bigr)[[X]]$$
--   from the quotient of the crossing model by the ideal generated by `U π` onto the one-variable power series ring over the quotient ring $W/(\pi)$, subject to three normalisations: $e$ sends the class of `V π` to the power series variable $\mathrm{PowerSeries.X}$, it sends the class of `U π` to $0$, and for every $w \in W$ it sends the class of `const π w` to the constant power series $\mathrm{PowerSeries.C}$ of the residue of $w$ in $W/(\pi)$. Thus the isomorphism is specified on the branch coordinate and on constants, not merely asserted abstractly.
--
--   This is the local algebraic form of the statement that, modulo one branch coordinate, the crossing model $W[[u,v]]/(uv-\pi)$ becomes a smooth one-dimensional formal germ over the residue ring $W/(\pi)$: killing $u$ forces $\pi = 0$ and leaves $(W/(\pi))[[v]]$. It is the basic computation behind the two-branch analysis of crossing presentations, and is used by the results on crossing presentations of complete local rings and on the identification of localised models in the Deligne–Rapoport description of the special fibre of the modular curve of level $\Gamma_0(p)$, whose components are copies of the smooth modular curve meeting transversally at the supersingular points.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_UVCrossingModel_exists_ringEquiv_quotient_span_U_powerSeries.lean

import Mathlib
